    "id": "chapter-5",
    "number": 5,
    "title": "Build Distribution Through Social Media",
    "paragraphs": [
      "16. Why distribution matters\nA useful product cannot help people who never discover it.",
      "Distribution is the bridge between your work and the people who may benefit from it. For a beginner with limited capital, organic social media can be a practical starting point because you can publish ideas without buying inventory or immediately paying for advertising.",
      "This does not mean social media is free in every sense. It requires time, attention, skill, and consistency. Platform reach is also not guaranteed.",
      "The strategic advantage is that you can test ideas, positioning, and offers in public before committing substantial money to paid acquisition.\n17. Start with one short-form platform\nChoose one platform that fits your preferred way of communicating and the audience you want to reach.",
      "Possible options include:\n- X for short posts and longer written articles.\n- LinkedIn for professional and business audiences.\n- Instagram for visual posts, carousels, and short videos.\n- YouTube for video and long-form education.\n- Substack for newsletters and a combination of short and long-form writing.\nThe source particularly favours a writing-led platform for people who enjoy deep thought and do not want to appear on camera. Its reasoning is that a newsletter relationship is more portable than a social follower relationship when the platform allows you to export subscriber information. Platform features and terms can change, so verify current export and portability options before relying on them.",
      "Do not start everywhere at once. Pick one channel, learn its formats, understand its audience, and become good at communicating there.\n18. Combine short-form and long-form content\nShort-form content can help people discover you. Long-form content gives you room to explain ideas, demonstrate depth, and build a stronger relationship.",
      "A simple system:\n- Publish short-form posts regularly.\n- Publish one substantial article, newsletter, or video each week.\n- Use the long-form piece as a source for additional short-form ideas.\n- Promote the relevant product or service in a useful, non-intrusive way.\n- Use audience responses to plan future work.\nLong-form content can increase the amount of time people spend with your ideas. That repeated attention may help them understand your perspective more deeply than a brief post alone.\n19. A manageable daily content routine\nThe source proposes a writing-led routine that can be adapted to your schedule.\nBlock 1 — Short-form writing (about 30 minutes)\nUse this time to:\n1. Research or review useful material.\n2. Choose one idea.\n3. Connect it to an audience problem or benefit.\n4. Draft a post.\n5. Edit and publish.\nDo not wait for the perfect idea. Writing is part of how you refine an idea. The goal is to practise making useful thoughts clear.\nBlock 2 — Long-form writing (about 30 minutes)\nWork on a newsletter, article, or video script.",
      "At the beginning of the week:\n- Choose the main topic.\n- Brainstorm possible titles.\n- Select the ideas to include.\n- Choose a structure.\n- Build an outline.\nThen use short daily sessions to develop the piece. The source suggests approximately 250 words as a manageable daily contribution, but the appropriate amount depends on your format and available time.\nBlock 3 — Offer promotion\nMake the offer visible regularly. This can be:\n- A relevant mention in a newsletter.\n- A contextual link in a post.\n- A product demonstration.\n- A call to action in a story or carousel.\n- A reply that helps someone with the relevant problem.\n- A direct invitation to learn more.\nPromotion should be connected to the subject being discussed. Avoid inserting irrelevant sales messages into every conversation.\n20. The Problem–Amplify–Solution promotion framework\nA simple promotional structure is:\n1. Problem: Identify the situation the audience is facing.\n2. Amplify: Explain why it matters or what happens if it remains unresolved.\n3. Solution: Present your product or service as a relevant way to address it.\nExample:\n- Problem: “You spend hours collecting research but struggle to turn it into a useful article.”\n- Amplify: “The notes keep growing, but the actual writing never gets easier because there is no process for selecting and organising ideas.”\n- Solution: “I created a research-to-outline workflow that helps you turn source material into a structured first draft.”\nThe example should be adapted to a real offer and a genuine problem. Do not exaggerate consequences or imply the product solves more than it does."
    ]
