    "id": "chapter-9",
    "number": 9,
    "title": "Your Weekly Operating System",
    "paragraphs": [
      "41. The basic weekly rhythm\nA simple one-person content business can operate on a repeating weekly cycle.\nStart of the week\n- Review the brand strategy and current business priority.\n- Research audience questions and relevant content examples.\n- Choose one long-form topic.\n- Brainstorm titles and create an outline.\n- Choose short-form themes for the week.\nDuring the week\n- Publish short-form content consistently.\n- Develop the long-form piece in manageable sessions.\n- Respond to relevant audience questions.\n- Promote the offer in context.\n- Record useful ideas and customer feedback.\nEnd of the week\n- Publish or finalise the long-form piece.\n- Review content performance.\n- Review enquiries and sales.\n- Note what people found useful or confusing.\n- Update the idea bank and AI workflows.\n- Choose the next experiment.\n42. A practical daily checklist\n- [ ] Review or research one useful idea.\n- [ ] Draft and publish a short-form post.\n- [ ] Make progress on the weekly long-form piece.\n- [ ] Engage with relevant people and conversations.\n- [ ] Make one contextual offer promotion, where appropriate.\n- [ ] Capture new ideas and questions.\n- [ ] Record useful feedback.\nThe source suggests publishing one to three short-form posts per day and one long-form piece per week. Treat this as a possible cadence, not a rule. Consistency and quality matter more than forcing a schedule that causes burnout or weak work.\n43. Track business outcomes, not just attention\nUseful indicators include:",
      "Audience\n- Relevant profile visits.\n- Followers or subscribers.\n- Email list growth.\n- Returning readers.\n- Replies and conversations.\nContent\n- Saves and shares.\n- Completion or reading behaviour where available.\n- Topics that repeatedly attract the right audience.\n- Content that generates useful questions.\nCommercial\n- Offer page visits.\n- Enquiries.\n- Conversion rate.\n- Sales.\n- Refunds.\n- Repeat purchases.\n- Customer feedback.\nDo not confuse views with revenue or follower count with customer demand. The purpose of distribution is to reach people who may value your offer."
    ]
